          "leadTitle": null,
          "bodyMarkdown": "**Schedule of defaults.** No default in this charter is derived from evidence; each is a judgement, and each is adjustable by ordinary proposal unless its clause says otherwise. Some are better accounted for than others, and this schedule says which. Quarterly review of domain weightings (2.4) is short enough to correct a bad weighting inside a year and long enough that weights are not a standing argument; any period under a year would satisfy the first half of that, and the quarter is chosen to sit alongside the other quarterly cycles rather than add a sixth rhythm. Delegation expiring after a year, and after ninety days on critical decisions (3.3), is set so a delegation survives ordinary inattention but not indifference. The ten per cent renewal share and twenty per cent review share (3.6) are judgements about when delegated support has stopped being one Member's trusted reach and become a standing block: ten per cent is the figure Article IX's health criterion already uses for the largest holder, twenty is double it, and the ten-supporter floor is the same floor every participation requirement carries, so that a sector of three delegations is never called concentrated. Minimum participation (3.5) is accounted for at 3.5a. The five per cent installation threshold (4.3) confers eligibility only — a Position decision still has to vote — which is why it carries no absolute companion as 4.7 does; five per cent is set low because the threshold opens a question rather than settling one. The two-supporter condition (4.7) is accounted for at 4.8a. The magnitudes beneath it are the thinnest-evidenced numbers in this article and are stated as such. The sustaining floor of three per cent is three fifths of the installation threshold, so a Position stops being held automatically — and a confirmation vote opens under 4.8 — once support falls below three fifths of what would newly open the question; automatic loss comes lower still, below the recall trigger of one and a half per cent (4.9), which is half the floor again. Both ratios describe the threshold and not the Delegate: 4.3's five per cent is eligibility to be installed, never the support any particular Delegate holds, so a Delegate installed with far more than five per cent can shed most of it and still sit above the floor with no review. That gap is real and these clauses do not close it; annual expiry under 3.3 is what eventually does. Both magnitudes are chosen, neither is derived, and a deployment with real delegation data should expect to move them. The fourteen-day grace period (4.9) is two weekends, on the reasoning that rebuilding support is work people do when they are not at work. The two per cent tie margin (4.10) is proportional deliberately and does not begin to bind until a sector holds roughly fifty delegations — below that only an exact tie is a tie. The accrual cap (5.3) is accounted for at 5.3a. Fourteen days for a panel to decide (6.2) is one working fortnight, long enough to read a record and exchange views without the dispute becoming the panel's second job; the forty-five and sixty days of amendment deliberation (8.1) are accounted for in Rules of Procedure 7.4, which gives the reasoning the window lengths actually rest on. None of the three is derived, and the distance between fourteen days and sixty is a judgement about how a panel's work differs from an amendment's, not a calibration of either. An Active Member is one with a verified contribution in the trailing ninety days (9.1) — a quarter, chosen so that “active” tracks what a Member is doing rather than what they once did. A Member away for a full season does lapse, and becomes Active again on their next verified contribution; the status is a description, not a sanction, and nothing in these documents treats losing it as a penalty. The Phase I and Phase II criteria (9.2, 9.3) are a judgement about what a community running without its founder looks like, not a measurement of one, because no such measurement exists — and, apart from 9.3's limit on the founder's share of completed proposals, what they measure is activity rather than independence. The Member counts, completed lifecycles and delegation movements they require could in principle all be produced by one operator running many verified accounts, which is the single thing they exist to rule out. They should be among the first defaults revisited against real evidence. They are not, however, the most consequential numbers here: both are amendable under 9.6, and 9.5 lets the community end founding authority at any time without either being met. The most consequential chosen number in these documents is the thirty-Member activation gate of Treasury Code 5.1, because charter 5.4 makes what it triggers permanent — the one default whose consequences a later community cannot amend away. Treasury Code 5.1a accounts for it, and says plainly that nothing in the evidence fixes it at thirty. The ten per cent delegation concentration ceiling of 9.3 is chosen too, and it sits awkwardly with a deployment carrying few sectors: the Triarc's own list defines three Positions (Triarc Operations Code 3.1), so meeting a ten per cent ceiling requires delegated trust to spread well beyond its Delegates rather than concentrating on them. That may be the right pressure or the wrong one. It is not a measurement either.",
